<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
      https://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.20.xsd">

  <!--
    MVP M-02: `role` was carrying two unrelated meanings — the permission tier (§12) and the job
    function (has capacity, takes object assignments, receives a workload share). That made the two
    mutually exclusive: promoting an engineer to editor dropped them out of GET /engineers and out
    of the assignment guard, while their object_engineers rows stayed behind and kept dividing the
    object's FTE. `is_engineer` splits the job function off so the two vary independently.
  -->
  <changeSet id="v1.1.1-1" author="a.semenas">
    <comment>Add is_engineer to users, separating job function from permission role</comment>

    <addColumn tableName="users">
      <column name="is_engineer" type="boolean" defaultValueBoolean="false">
        <constraints nullable="false"/>
      </column>
    </addColumn>

    <rollback>
      <dropColumn tableName="users" columnName="is_engineer"/>
    </rollback>
  </changeSet>

  <!--
    Backfill: before this migration, role='engineer' was the only way to be an engineer, so every
    such account is one. Split from the DDL changeset so a re-run of the backfill is independently
    tracked, and so the rollback restores the pre-migration state exactly.
  -->
  <changeSet id="v1.1.1-2" author="a.semenas">
    <comment>Backfill is_engineer from the existing engineer role</comment>

    <update tableName="users">
      <column name="is_engineer" valueBoolean="true"/>
      <where>role = 'engineer'</where>
    </update>

    <rollback>
      <update tableName="users">
        <column name="is_engineer" valueBoolean="false"/>
        <where>role = 'engineer'</where>
      </update>
    </rollback>
  </changeSet>

  <!--
    The engineer *role* is the self-scoped permission tier — own row, own objects — which is only
    meaningful for someone who is actually an engineer. An account holding that role without the
    flag would be invisible in GET /engineers while still owning object assignments, which is the
    exact failure this migration exists to prevent. The reverse is allowed and is the point: an
    engineer may hold the editor or admin role. Runs after the backfill so pre-existing rows pass.
  -->
  <changeSet id="v1.1.1-3" author="a.semenas">
    <comment>Constrain the engineer role to accounts flagged as engineers</comment>

    <sql>
      ALTER TABLE users ADD CONSTRAINT ck_users_engineer_role
        CHECK (role &lt;&gt; 'engineer' OR is_engineer = true)
    </sql>

    <rollback>
      <sql>ALTER TABLE users DROP CONSTRAINT ck_users_engineer_role</sql>
    </rollback>
  </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
